            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="FAL03" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>J. Scott</forename>
                        <surname>Fallowfield</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1000-01-01">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1000-12-31"/>
                    <occupation>OCCUPATION</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p> There are three contenders, two of whom might be identical. The most
                            probably is the Fallowfield, who, with Turton and Snodgrass was a
                            truusteee of the Drury Lane Theatre. THis might be J. Scott Fallowfield,
                            a Director of the Life Assurance Society, but they may be distinct. It
                            seems unlikely to be Henry Fallowfield, Rev'd, who appears at the King's
                            levy on a number of occasions in the period.</p> <p> The connection with Drury Lane seems apt given that Godwin meets Fallowfield at the house of Francis Reynolds, the dramatist.
                            There is a Fallowfield who is a printer and bookseller in this period in Preston, Lancs.</p>
                    </note>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
